+**What is normative in ``validate_report_schema``** (enforced, fail-closed):
+
+* the registered ``OpDefinition`` identity (``resolved.contract``) and the
+  canonical ``layout_decision`` re-derivation (including the alias proof);
+* the policy digest recomputed from ``requested_policy``;
+* cross-block digest/provider pairs: ``resolution.capability_digest`` vs
+  ``capability.snapshot_digest``, ``resolution.contract_digest`` vs
+  ``resolved.contract.digest``, ``dispatch.provider_declared`` vs
+  ``resolution.fallback_chain[0]``, ``resolved.provider`` vs
+  ``dispatch.provider_invoked``, ``dispatch.fallback_used`` vs
+  ``resolution.fallback_used``;
+* every *named* ``resolution.rules[*].details`` echo, mapped declaratively by
+  rule name + key to its authoritative copy (request block, capability /
+  resolved target, requested / resolved policy, ``layout_decision``,
+  candidates/rejections) -- missing, duplicated or unknown rules fail closed;
+* the **selected** candidate's stable-name checks: ``rank.requested`` equals
+  the request rank and ``dtype.declared`` contains ``request.dtype`` (or is
+  ``contract_domain`` and the registered contract contains it);
+* fallback chain structure: non-empty, unique, ``chain[chain_position]`` is
+  the invoked provider;
+* coverage opcode/provider/artifact-format copies, portable artifact version
+  constants, and contract-derived guarantees (``accumulation``,
+  ``exactness_basis``, ``reference``, ``rounding``, ``scope``, determinism
+  chain).
+
+**What is an explanation layer, not normative** (no in-document ground truth
+is enforced): positional ``resolution.candidates[*].checks[*]`` (except the
+selected candidate's ``rank``/``dtype`` anchors above) and
+``resolution.rejections[*].failed_checks[*].detail`` payloads;
+``resolution.why``; coverage counts/flags/``parent_opcode``;
+``dispatch.*_reconciled`` flags; ``gates.*``; free-text
+``timing.timing_basis.*``; ``rejections[*].library_version``/``message``;
+``artifact.content_digest``; ``resolution.plan_digest``;
+``resolution.degraded`` (report-level runtime fact); ``thread_state.
+loaded_threads``; non-selected providers' ``deterministic``; and the
+manifest-only facts (library fingerprint, pack strategy value, vendor
+artifact versions, ``guarantees.reduction_order``/``reduction_order_id``).
+
+**Irreducible single-document limit**: a forger who rewrites *every* copy of
+a fact that has no external truth in the document (request dtype/shape,
+capability features, manifest-only facts) can still produce a self-consistent
+report.  Platform features and provider manifests are not embedded, so
+``validate_report_schema`` cannot act as an execution authorization.
+``validate_report_schema`` runs in ``ExecutionReport.__init__`` **after**
+execution; no execution path consumes a report, and a downstream consumer
+must not re-plan from a report.  Execution authorization remains
+``execute_matmul_plan`` -> :func:`pypto.execution.entry._verify_plan_binding`,
+which re-runs the resolver against the digest-checked capability snapshot.
+
+The boundary list is mirrored in
+``docs/10-architecture/0006-...`` section 7.1 and
+``_meta/pypto-x/view-mode-require/brief.zh-CN.md`` section 14.
